<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Release entitlements: aps-environment is "production".

  ## Why this is a second file rather than one shared with Debug

  APNs has two entirely separate environments with separate device tokens. A token
  minted under "development" is meaningless to the production APNs endpoint and vice
  versa — the send is accepted and the notification simply never arrives. Nothing warns
  you, on either side.

  Xcode does rewrite this key during Organizer export, so a single "development" file
  usually survives an App Store build. That is behaviour, not a contract: it does not
  apply to a plain `xcodebuild archive`, and it is invisible when it stops applying.
  Splitting the file makes the value a build-configuration fact instead — Debug builds
  onto a device get a sandbox token, Release/TestFlight/App Store builds get a
  production one, and neither depends on an export step doing a substitution.

  This must agree with APNS_ENV on the server (see .env.example and server/lib/apns.ts),
  which defaults to the sandbox. A TestFlight or App Store build needs
  APNS_ENV=production set on Railway, or the server will sign for the wrong environment
  and every notification will be silently dropped.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>production</string>
</dict>
</plist>
